Dust constitutes only a small fraction (∼\sim1%) of the interstellar medium (ISM), yet it plays a crucial role in various processes within galaxies (Draine 2011), including heating the gas through the photoelectric effect (Draine 1978), cooling gas via infrared emission, providing surfaces for molecular gas formation, and shielding molecules from dissociating radiation (Gould and Salpeter 1963; Hollenbach and Salpeter 1971). Understanding the dust properties within galaxies is therefore essential for studying the life cycle of gas in the ISM, complementing studies of the multiphase gas based on tracers such as H i, CO, and [C i] (Li and Goldsmith 2003; Bolatto et al. 2013; Li et al. 2018; Yu et al. 2024; Xia et al. 2026, e.g.,).

