Studies of nearby galaxies can overcome these challenges (Galliano et al. 2018; Tabatabaei et al. 2014; Ejlali et al. 2025; Pantoni et al. 2026, e.g.,), avoiding the foreground/background confusion and distance ambiguities inherent to Galactic observations. The Andromeda galaxy (M31), located at a distance of 766±\pm18 kpc (Lee 2023), is the nearest massive spiral galaxy. Its proximity provides an opportunity to study the dust properties in an external spiral galaxy at high spatial resolution with millimeter single-dish telescopes. Extensive infrared and submillimeter observations of M31 have been obtained with IRAS (Habing et al. 1984; Devereux et al. 1994), the Infrared Space Observatory (Haas et al. 1998), the Spitzer Space Telescope (Gordon et al. 2006), Herschel Space Observatory (Fritz et al. 2012; Groves et al. 2012; Draine et al. 2014), Planck space telescope (Planck Collaboration et al. 2015), and JCMT-SCUBA2 (Smith et al. 2021; Jiao et al. 2026). Based on these observations, the dust properties across the disk have been studied in detail (Smith et al. 2012; Draine et al. 2014; Planck Collaboration et al. 2015; Jiao et al. 2026, e.g.,). In many of these analyses, the dust emission is modeled using a single modified blackbody, Sν=Ω​Bν​(Td)​(1−e−τν)S_{
