However, the reported dust property distributions of M31 in previous works are mixed, especially regarding radial trends in β eta (Smith et al. 2012; Draine et al. 2014; Planck Collaboration et al. 2015; Whitworth et al. 2019; Athikkat-Eknath et al. 2022; Jiao et al. 2026, e.g.,). Based on Herschel observations, Smith et al. (2012) identified a sharp change in β eta at R≈\approx3 kpc, with β eta rising from 2 to 2.5 within 3 kpc and then decreasing from 2.5 to 1.5 from ∼\sim3 kpc to the ∼\sim 15 kpc radii. Mattsson et al. (2014) proposed that substantial interstellar dust grain growth might account for this apparent drop in β eta. In contrast, Draine et al. (2014) derived β eta from Herschel SPIRE500/SPIRE250 band ratios and found β≈2.08 eta\approx 2.08 for R≳7R\gtrsim 7 kpc, consistent with the dust model in Draine and Li (2007). Including JCMT-SCUBA2 450 and 850 μ\mum observations in the SED fitting, Jiao et al. (2026) reported smaller variations in β eta (1.5 ≲\lesssim β eta ≲\lesssim 2.3), especially for R≲\lesssim7 kpc. The results show an average β eta value of 1.8, which is smaller than values reported in other studies, such as, Draine et al. (2014), where β eta was defined in a post-processing step, between 250 and 500 μ\mum, and Whitworth et al. (2019), where β eta was treated as an intrinsic parameter in the PPMAP (Marsh et al. 2015) analysis. These discrepancies may arise in the differences in wavelength coverage and fitting methodology.

